#--: Introduction

This document is the architectural map of the zerolang typechecker.
It is intended to be read #_*"before" opening
#~"src/ztypecheck.z" -- after reading it,
a contributor should know which structures the typechecker
maintains, how its code is partitioned, and where a particular
feature lives.

The companion document #~"compiler.pdoc" covers #_*"semantics" --
what ownership means, how the multi-granularity lock-conflict rule
works, what demand-driven resolution guarantees. This document
covers #_*"structure": data layout, dispatch, code organisation.
The two documents are deliberately split so that semantic rules
have a single source of truth (compiler.pdoc / ownership.pdoc) and
this one stays a navigation aid that does not rot when rule wording
changes.

#b:"Audience." A new contributor who has already read
#~"spec.pdoc", #~"ownership.pdoc", and the Semantic-Analyser
section of #~"compiler.pdoc".


#--: Pipeline Position

The typechecker sits between the parser and the C emitter:

#code language=text: """
  zast.ProgramData  (frozen post-parse)
      ↓   typecheck(program)
  ztyping.ZTyping  (mutable, holds all typecheck output)
      ↓
  zemitterc / zsqldump
"""

The parser hands the typechecker a #~"zast.ProgramData" -- an immutable
tree of #~"Node" values, each with a monotonic #~"nodeid". The
typechecker does not mutate AST nodes; instead it writes its results
into a single mutable container, #~"ztyping.ZTyping", keyed by
#~"nodeid". After #~"typecheck()" returns, the emitter and SQL dumper
read from both the #~"ProgramData" (shape) and the #~"ZTyping" (types,
ownership, locks, monomorphisations) without further analysis.

#b:"Demand-driven, single pass." Resolution starts at #~"main" and
descends only into names that are actually referenced. Names that
are never reached are never typechecked. The same property gives
cycle detection for free -- a name that is encountered while it is
already on the resolution stack is reported as a circular
dependency. See #~"compiler.pdoc:Demand-Driven Resolution" for the
rationale.


#--: Core Data Structures

The typechecker is built around four core record types, the
declaration tree, and one mutable output container. Reading this
section first makes #~"ztypecheck.z" considerably easier to follow,
because nearly every function in it produces, mutates, or consults one
of these.


#---: ZType -- the unified type descriptor

#~"ZType" (in #~"src/ztypes.z") is the #_*"only" type
representation in the compiler. There is no separate "kind",
"shape", or "schema" object: every type -- record, class, variant,
union, protocol, facet, function, unit, numeric, native -- is a
#~"ZType" with a #~"typetype" discriminator (the #~"ztypetype"
enum) and a set of optional fields.

Key fields:

#ul:
  [#*: #~"name": the simple name (e.g. #~"Point")]
  [#*: #~"typetype": the #~"ztypetype" enum
    (#~"RECORD", #~"CLASS", #~"VARIANT", #~"UNION", #~"PROTOCOL",
    #~"FACET", #~"FUNCTION", #~"UNIT", numerics, ...)]
  [#*: #~"returnTypeId", #~"thisParamNameId":
    populated for function types]
  [#*: generic-state fields: #~"genericOriginId",
    #~"isTagGenericOrigin", and the specialization trie
    #~"genericChildren" (an #~"IdMap" keyed by the argument's type id,
    or by the literal value at a numeric parameter level) with the
    ordered argument ids in #~"genericArgs" / #~"genericArgIsValue"]
  [#*: layout / memory metadata: #~"needsDestructor",
    #~"isHeapAllocated", #~"isBox" (see
    #~"representation_of_types.pdoc")]
  [#*: #~"ownerTid": the type owning this one as a member, or 0.
    The emitter composes C names from it; nothing else reads it]
  [#*: #~"def": the #~"Decl" that minted the type, or 0]
  [#*: #~"typeId": monotonic id for SQL dumps]

A #~"ZType" carries identity-data only. #_*"Per-member" metadata --
parameter ownership annotations, field defaults, the lock-field and
function-pointer-slot marks -- lives on the member's own #~"Decl",
not on the parent #~"ZType". Member #_*"visibility" is likewise
structural, held by the type's #~"publicNs" declaration subtree.

A member's declaration must therefore exist #_*"before" any metadata
write, which is why every synthesis site declares the member and then
types it, in that order: #~"declSetMemberType" stamps the member's type
onto its declaration, and a method's declaration is what claims its own
function type, which every later parameter write reaches through.


#---: ZVariable -- per-binding ownership carrier

#~"ZVariable" (#~"src/ztypes.z") is one runtime variable: a row of
the registry's #~"variableById", its vid the row index (row 0 is the
sentinel). A variable is minted only for #_*"named runtime bindings"
-- function parameters, locals introduced by assignment,
#~"with"-bindings, for-loop iterators, and synth temps -- and referenced
from #~"Decl.varId"; type and function definitions carry no variable.
#~"newVariable" mints the row and returns the vid; the getters and
setters on the registry (#~"variableTypeOf", #~"variableOwnershipOf",
#~"setVariableBorrowOrigin", ...) are the only access.

#ul:
  [#*: #~"ztypeId": the variable's resolved type]
  [#*: #~"ownership": #~"OWNED" or #~"BORROWED" (the
    2-state model described in #~"compiler.pdoc:Ownership and
    Lifetime")]
  [#*: #~"isPrivateAccess": carried through #~".private"
    typedef projections]
  [#*: #~"borrowOrigin": the name id of the root of the path this
    variable borrows from; 0 for an owned variable and for parameters]
  [#*: #~"synthOrigin": pass-name string for variables
    synthesised by a compiler pass (e.g. #~"\"anf\"" for hoisted
    call temps); none for user-source variables]
  [#*: #~"origin": how the variable came to be -- a parameter, a
    local binding, or synthesised]

Keeping ownership off #~"ZType" lets the same type be bound in two
scopes with different ownership, which is the common case.


#---: ZEntry -- an overlay row

#~"ZEntry" (#~"src/ztypes.z") is one overlay row of the body walk: a
take, a narrowing, or a lock laid over a local while the frame that laid
it is open. A local itself is a #~"localDecl" in the declaration tree;
the row points at it.

#ul:
  [#*: #~"entryId": monotonic id]
  [#*: #~"declId" (the local's declaration -- its name, type and
    variable are read off it), #~"defSeq" (the row's place among the
    frame's locals, for the dump)]
  [#*: #~"ztypeId": the type the row presents (a narrowing changes it)]
  [#*: #~"hasLock", #~"lockType", #~"lockScope", #~"lockHolder",
    #~"lockPath": lock state -- the lock kind, whether it is on the leaf or
    an intent on a prefix, its holder, and the addressable path as name
    ids #~"(root, f1, f2, ...)"]
  [#*: #~"narrowedSubtype", #~"excludedSubtypes": type narrowing for
    union / variant subjects in match arms, as arm name ids]
  [#*: #~"originalZtypeId": the un-narrowed type, so the
    emitter can generate the C-level unwrap]
  [#*: #~"isTaken", #~"takenAt": consumed-binding state]

Rows live on #~"ZSymbolTable.entries" and die with their frame; the
local's declaration persists, so the walk can return to it.


#---: ZTyping -- the typecheck-output container

#~"ZTyping" (#~"src/ztyping.z") is the single mutable container
holding everything the typechecker computes. It is intentionally
ECS-shaped: instead of decorating parsed AST nodes with extra
columns, the typechecker writes into a set of per-#~"nodeid"
component maps.

The declaration tree lives here: #~"decls" (every #~"Decl" row, id =
index, row 0 the sentinel; see #~"src/ztyping.z"), #~"declByTypeId",
#~"publicNsByOwner", #~"refDecl" (reference node -> resolved declaration),
#~"pendingDefs" (the demand queue) and the unit-root anchors
(#~"programRoot", #~"coreUnitRoot").

Per-node stamps (each keyed by parsed-node #~"nodeid"): #~"nodeType"
(dense) and #~"nodeConst" / #~"memberConst" (the compile-time value
domain), #~"callKind", #~"atomVariableId", #~"implicitCreate", the
dotted-path stamps (#~"dpTaggedParent", #~"dpArmPredicate",
#~"dpTagRead"), the post-guard and branch-fold stamps
(#~"postGuard*", #~"foldedBranch", #~"truthyCondArm"), the per-variable
facts (#~"variableMutated", #~"variableMoved"), the
2-arm-sum facts (#~"sumPairFirstArm" / #~"sumPairSecondArm"), and the
per-instantiation mono stamps (#~"monoNodeType", #~"monoBinopOwner").
The class comment in #~"src/ztyping.z" is the authoritative list. The
container also carries some of the body walk's ambient state (the
pending-borrow slot, the suppress flags), the error accumulator
(#~"errors", #~"isError"), the conformance rows and the build facts
(#~"ccPath", #~"ccMode", #~"targetTriple").

The SQL dumper writes one row per component entry of the tables it
projects (#~"src/zsqldump.z").
The SQL dumper writes one row per component entry, so every table
has a direct SQL representation.


#---: ZSymbolTable -- the body walk's state

#~"ZSymbolTable" (#~"src/zenv.z") is the body walk's state over the
declaration tree. A frame is a #~"blockDecl" under the frame or
declaration enclosing it and a local is a #~"localDecl" under the frame
it is written in, so both persist and can be returned to; the table holds
what is transient: the stack of open frames (#~"open", innermost last,
each an #~"openframe" with the frame's row, its parent, and where its
overlay rows begin),
the overlay rows laid over locals (#~"entries", see ZEntry above), the
frames whose remainder proved unreachable, and the rows snapshotted for the
dump when a frame closes. It owns the type registry and the typing tables
outright, so every method reads metadata through #~"this.reg" /
#~"this.typing".

A local resolves by climbing the open frame chain; a frame's parent that
is not a frame -- the function declaration -- ends the climb, so two
functions never see each other's locals whatever order their bodies are
walked in.

The frame a resolver works in is an explicit #~"frame:" argument, never
an ambient cursor: a declaration resolves in its parent's frame
(#~"declParentIdOf"), a body walk opens its base frame under the
declaration (#~"push name: parent:"), and every resolver it reaches is
handed the frame it should resolve from. The table's #~"curFrame" is the
body walk's own position -- the innermost open frame that has a row --
and only #~"pushFrame" / #~"materialiseTop" / #~"pop" move it. Key APIs:

#ul:
  [#*: #~"pushFrame" / #~"pushBlock" / #~"pushCall" / #~"pop" / #~"popTo"
    -- open and close frames (a block frame that declares nothing mints no
    row)]
  [#*: #~"defineVar" / #~"define" / #~"lookup" / #~"lookupVarId"
    -- local definition and name -> type / variable resolution]
  [#*: #~"tryLock" -- attempt to install a lock; returns the
    installed lock or a conflict description. The multi-granularity
    rule is in #~"lockAcquireConflict"]
  [#*: #~"findExclusiveLock", #~"isPathLocked" -- access-
    site enforcement (used by lock-escape checks)]
  [#*: #~"narrow", #~"exclude" -- install a narrowing or
    exclusion overlay row in the current frame]

The lock conflict logic lives in #~"zenv.z", not in
#~"ztypecheck.z". The typechecker only chooses #_*"when" to
install a lock; the rule for whether it conflicts is owned by
the scope module.


#--: Code Map of #~"ztypecheck.z"

The file is long, but it organises top-to-bottom roughly as
follows. Functions are listed by name (no line numbers -- they
rot fast; #~"grep -n" finds them quickly). Use this map to scroll
into the right region; read prose below for the semantics.

#table:
  [#tr: [#th: Band] [#th: Key functions]]
  [#tr: [#td: Pass setup]
        [#td: module-level #~"typecheck" entry -- resolving
              stack, unit cache, call-id stack, and the
              fresh-temp counter (#~"ZTyping.nextTempId")]]
  [#tr: [#td: Entry point]
        [#td: #~"typecheck" -- starts at #~"main", drives demand-
              driven resolution and body checking, returns the
              accumulated error list]]
  [#tr: [#td: Unit-level name resolution]
        [#td: #~"resolveDef" (dispatches over #~"nodetype"),
              #~"resolveUnitBody"]]
  [#tr: [#td: Type-definition resolvers]
        [#td: #~"resolveFunction",
              #~"resolveRecord", #~"resolveClass",
              #~"resolveUnion",
              #~"resolveVariant",
              #~"resolveProtocol",
              #~"resolveFacet",
              #~"resolveInlineGenericUnit",
              #~"resolveData"]]
  [#tr: [#td: Name and typeref resolution]
        [#td: #~"envLookup", #~"walkLookupTyperef",
              #~"resolveTyperefShell", #~"checkDotted",
              #~"dottedParentName", and the
              #~"type" / #~"this" keyword resolvers]]
  [#tr: [#td: Monomorphisation]
        [#td: #~"getOrMintSpec", #~"mintMono",
              #~"validateGenericTypeArgs"]]
  [#tr: [#td: Function body]
        [#td: #~"checkFunctionBody", #~"checkMethodBodies",
              #~"checkBodies"]]
  [#tr: [#td: Statements]
        [#td: #~"checkStatement",
              #~"checkStmtInner",
              #~"checkStatementLine",
              #~"checkAssignment",
              #~"checkReassignment", #~"checkSwap"]]
  [#tr: [#td: Expressions]
        [#td: #~"checkExpression", #~"checkValue",
              #~"checkAtomid",
              #~"checkString"]]
  [#tr: [#td: Dotted paths]
        [#td: #~"checkDotted" -- auto-call coercion gate;
              handles #~".take" / #~".release" / #~".borrow" /
              #~".view" / #~".private"]]
  [#tr: [#td: Calls]
        [#td: #~"checkCall",
              #~"checkMissingCallArgs",
              #~"lockCallArg", #~"installBorrowLocks",
              #~"checkProjectionCreate",
              #~"checkTypedefCreate",
              #~"checkTypedefBorrow", and the argument-take /
              lock-escape helpers]]
  [#tr: [#td: Special call forms]
        [#td: #~"checkBinop" and the return-call
              (#~"children[create]" construction) and
              string-convert dispatch]]
  [#tr: [#td: Control flow]
        [#td: #~"checkIf",
              #~"checkFor", #~"checkWith",
              #~"checkCase" / #~"checkClause" (match arm checking,
              including branch narrowing and post-match
              exclusion), #~"checkBoxConstruction"]]


#--: Dispatch Mechanism

Per the project convention (#~"CLAUDE.md"), the typechecker
dispatches on the #~"nodetype" discriminator rather than runtime
type tests. The pattern appears in two forms:

#ul:
  [#*: #b:"Closed dispatch keyed by #~"nodetype"" -- e.g. the
    definition resolvers. Used where the set of cases is closed
    and known up front (type-definition resolvers, statement-line
    kinds).]
  [#*: #b:"Inline match on" #~"node.nodetype" -- used inside
    #~"checkExpression", #~"checkValue", and a few similar
    high-fan-out helpers where a flat match reads more clearly
    than a table.]

After the discriminator check, #~"cast()" narrows the node to the
concrete #~"Node" arm; see #~"lib/system/zast.z".


#--: Resolution Stack and Cycle Detection

Resolution is keyed by #_*"declaration", not by synthesized dotted-string
names. Every definition is one Decl in the tree, found from a reference by
walking the frames outward from the frame the reference was written in,
and that Decl carries its own resolution state -- #~"pending" (demanded,
queued), #~"resolving" (grey), #~"defined" (its type id is final),
#~"walked" (its body checked). A reference that reaches a declaration
already #~"resolving" is a cycle back-edge: a signature reference needs
only the DECLARED id, so mutual references between signatures are legal,
and a reference that needs the definition complete reports E0023 /
E0100. The flow is:

#ol:
  [#*: A reference resolves to a Decl (#~"envLookup" for a bare name,
    the qualified walk for #~"unit.member"). Defined: return its id.]
  [#*: Not yet defined: mark it pending and queue it
    (#~"pendingDefs"); #~"resolveDemandedDefs" drains the queue from the
    top level, dispatching to the #~"resolve*" resolver by #~"nodetype"
    in the declaration's own frame.]
  [#*: A nested resolution that re-enters a #~"resolving" declaration
    takes the declared id (or reports the cycle).]
  [#*: On success the resolver marks the declaration #~"defined" and
    links its type (#~"declSetType" / #~"ZType.def").]

The #~"type" keyword (in receiver position) resolves to the type
at the bottom of the resolving stack, so a method body can refer
to its own enclosing type without naming it. #~"this" works
similarly.


#--: Type-name resolution and the canonical-id rule

A bare type name (the #~"i64" in #~"x: i64", a conformance marker, a
field or parameter type) resolves #_*"outward" from the frame it is
written in: the enclosing namespace or template, the current unit, then
the prelude (#~"core", whose members are re-exports of the system units).
First match wins, so a type defined in the current unit #_*"shadows" a
same-named stdlib builtin, and there is no list of stdlib unit names
anywhere in the resolver. Two functions implement this:

#ul:
  [#*: #~"envLookup(ty, frame, nameId)" -- the frame walk: which
    declaration #~"nameId" denotes for a reference written in #~"frame".
    #~"resolveAtomType" routes a name atom through it, get-or-declaring
    the ONE canonical id via #~"declareCanonicalType".]
  [#*: #~"walkLookupTyperef(ast, ty, reg, unitDecl, base)" -- the same
    walk for a type reference, answering a type id, with the declared id
    for a declaration still resolving.]

Cross-unit references are #_*"dotted" (#~"zast.Node", #~"io.File"),
resolved by the named unit; bare names never reach across units except
through the prelude. A unit #~"foo.z" with a private #~"foo/" directory
holds implementation subunits, loaded by the parser when referred to.

#b:"The canonical-id rule." A definition resolves #_*"once", into one
canonical type id keyed by its #~"defn.nodeid" (with #~"defined_in_unit"
recording the owning unit). That id #_*"is" the type's identity -- not
its name. Downstream passes #_*"trust" the locked id and never re-resolve
a type by its name: the C emitter derives a type's C form from the id
(via #~"typeRefC" / #~"scalarCTypeFor" / #~"cTypeForNameTid"), never from
the name, so a user type that shadows a builtin scalar
(#~"i64: record { ... }") emits #_*"its" struct, while the system #~"i64"
reached through the same name keeps the C scalar. Re-resolving by name
would collapse the two onto whichever the search-root unit wins -- the
bug class the #~"make shadow-guard" ratchet prevents from returning.

#b:"Mono identity follows the same rule." A generic instance is identified by
the specialization trie: each template holds its spec-tree edges in
#~"genericChildren", one level per parameter in definition order, keyed by
the argument's type id -- or, for an integer-scalar-constrained parameter
(#~"str to: 16"), by the literal VALUE itself (#~"genericArgIsValue" marks
such ordinals). Ids cannot collide across
same-named types, and the mono NAME (#~"Map_String_i64") remains only as the
display / C-name stem; a user shadow of a builtin still appends its canonical
id to that name (#~"monoValName": #~"optionval_i64_7") so generated wrapper
names stay distinct too.


#--: Unit members: three passes, each keyed on the value's ARM

A unit-level definition is dispatched three times, and every one of them
matches on the shape of the member's VALUE node. Reading only one of
them is how a shape ends up silently registering nothing:

#ul:
  [#*: #~"indexDeclDef" (the Decl tree) -- #~"functiondef" -> funcDecl,
    #~"objectdef" -> typeDecl, #~"unitdef" -> unitDecl, #~"datablock" ->
    dataDecl, #~"dottedpath" and a non-numeric #~"atomid" -> reexportDecl
    (the alias), everything else -> constDecl.]
  [#*: #~"resolveDef" (resolution) -- the same definition arms, plus the
    dotted alias leg; anything else is skipped.]
  [#*: #~"resolveConstantDef" (post-demand) -- the literal shapes:
    a numeric #~"atomid", a suffixed literal (#~"1.u32", a dotted path
    whose parent is the literal), a constant expression. A dotted value
    whose parent is neither a literal nor a unit, and a parenthesised
    call, are constructions and are refused here.]

Two consequences worth knowing before adding a leg. A definition's own
unit is NOT resolvable through the typeref walk while this runs, so a
same-file type or unit answers only through the Decl tree
(#~"declOfUnitDef", #~"declDefTypetype", the Decl's #~"kind") -- the
registry says "no". And #~"origin: Point.create x: 0" parses as TWO
members, the dotted value #~"Point.create" and a separate constant
#~"x: 0", so a construction never arrives as a call unless it is
parenthesised.

#--: The numeric id spaces

Every id the compiler carries is a zero-overhead typedef over its base
integer, so one space cannot silently stand in for another: equality is
same-space only and a sentinel reads #~"isZero" rather than #~"== 0".

#ul:
  [#*: #b:"nodeid" (#~"u32", #~"zast") -- one node in the single
    monotonic node space, and the index of its row in #~"Ast.nodes"
    (row #~"0" is the sentinel placeholder); a synthesized node reads
    #~"fsnoForNode == 0".]
  [#*: #b:"nameid" (#~"u32", #~"zast") -- an interned identifier's
    text in the #~"StringPool".]
  [#*: #b:"tid" / #b:"declid" / #b:"vid" (#~"u64", #~"ztypes") -- a
    type, a declaration-tree node, a bound variable.]
  [#*: #b:"fsno" (#~"zvfs") -- a source file.]

A typedef stands in for its base as a WHOLE VALUE, never as a
COMPONENT: a scalar slot (parameter, field, arm payload) carries the
typedef, while #_*"containers do not". A #~"(ListVal u32)" of child ids
and the u64-keyed stamp tables keep their base type, and the id narrows
(#~".u64", #~".u32") or is minted (#~"zast.nodeid.create from: ...") at
that boundary.

Residuals that stay bare by design, because the slot genuinely holds
more than one space: #~"genericChildren"'s key, #~"genericArgs",
#~"genericArgAt"'s return (a tid OR a numeric generic value),
#~"zlockholder.id" (a vid OR a node id), and the emitter's
#~"interpPartNodeId", whose consumers ARE the u64-keyed stamp tables. #~"zvfs"
carries its own #~"dentryid" / #~"providerid" pair, out of this scope.


#--: Cross-Cutting Concerns

Each subsection below points at #_*"where" a concern lives in the
typechecker. For #_*"why" it works that way, follow the links to
the semantic documents.


#---: Ownership enforcement

The 2-state ownership model (see #~"compiler.pdoc:Ownership and
Lifetime") is enforced at the assignment and call sites:

#ul:
  [#*: #~"checkAssignment" -- definition site. Records the
    new variable's ownership in a fresh #~"ZVariable", consumes
    the pending borrow by installing a borrow lock.]
  [#*: #~"checkReassignment" -- enforces that the LHS variable
    is currently #~"OWNED" and that the RHS provides the
    appropriate ownership; resets narrowing and re-runs lock
    escape checks.]
  [#*: #~"checkSwap" -- symmetric ownership preservation.]
  [#*: the argument-site #~".take" -- transfers ownership from
    the caller's binding into the callee's parameter.]

Parameters default to #~".borrow"; #~".take" and #~".view" are
the opt-in annotations. The annotations live on the parameter's own
#~"Decl", not on the parameter #~"ZType".

#b:"The `:name` shorthand at the argument site." A #~":name"
argument parses as a #~"labelvalue" node, not an #~"atomid", so
#~"walkCallArgsHoist"'s bare-name leg resolves it explicitly, and
from there it is the written-out #~"name: name" form: at a
#~".take" parameter the shorthand transfers ownership (the source
is invalidated; a later use is E0200), and at a lock/borrow
parameter it takes the same call-scoped lock through
#~"lockCallArg" (#~"EXCLUSIVE"/#~"SHARED"), so a readonly binding
passed via #~":name" to a mutable-borrow parameter is refused with
E0200 exactly as #~"b: b" is
(#~"tests/fixtures/errors/shorthand_arg_locks.z").


#---: Lock checking

The typechecker chooses #_*"when" to install a lock; #~"zenv.z"
owns the conflict rule (#~"lockAcquireConflict") and the
prefix-overlap path test (#~"pathsOverlap"). Two scopes of
locks, both described in #~"compiler.pdoc:Lock Checking":

#ul:
  [#*: #b:"Call-scoped" -- installed by #~"lockCallArg" inside a
    #~"CALL" scope that wraps argument processing. The leaf is
    #~"EXCLUSIVE" for mutable and owned markers but #~"SHARED" for a
    #~".view" argument (#~"lockCallArg" takes #~"shared:"); every
    intermediate prefix is #~"SHARED".]
  [#*: #b:"Borrow-scoped" -- installed by
    #~"installBorrowLocks" when a binding takes a borrow into
    a #~"BLOCK" scope (#~".borrow", #~".view", #~".stringview",
    #~"with", or a #~".borrow"-param transfer). Persists for the
    lifetime of the binding's scope; released by scope pop or
    explicit #~".release".]

The aggregate-lock-escape check runs at storage sites (field
writes) and at return sites; it consults #~"isPathLocked" on
the scope chain to reject a write/return that would let a locked
value escape its scope.

Self-overlap during a single call (e.g.
#~"f.method bv: f.byteview") is permitted via the call-identity
stack and #~"zlockholder" tagging: #~"tryLock"
skips the conflict check when the existing holder matches the
current call's identity.


#---: Borrow correctness for parameters

Deep methods that take a borrow into a return value record the
source path on the pending-borrow slot (#~"ZTyping.pendingBorrowPath"
and its flags); the binding site (#~"checkAssignment" or the
call-argument check) consumes it with #~"takePendingBorrow" and
installs the borrow lock against the actual destination frame, and
#~"drainPendingBorrow" clears it at statement and call boundaries so
it cannot leak across statements.
The dedicated #~".take" / #~".borrow" / #~".view" / #~".hold" /
#~".release" / #~".private" handling lives in #~"checkDotted".

#~"public:"/#~"private:" visibility is enforced from that same machinery.
#~"scanVisibility" (run per type in the resolvers) records fields whose
declared type is a #~".private" handle on the member declaration
(#~"Decl.grantsPrivate"); privacy itself is structural -- a member is public
exactly when the type's #~"publicNs" subtree reaches it. Two gates in
#~"checkDotted" then apply, both keyed on
#~"baseIsPrivateCapable" (is the base #~"this" / a same-type receiver, a
#~".private"-typed variable, or a #~".private"-typed field read): reading a
private member from a non-capable base raises E0101, and taking #~".private" of
a non-capable base raises E0102. Construction argument labels never reach
#~"checkDotted", so naming private fields at construction is unaffected.


#---: Monomorphisation

Generic types and functions are monomorphised by deep-cloning
the original AST subtree, binding the generic parameters to
concrete types, and re-running the standard resolvers on the
clone.

#ul:
  [#*: #~"getOrMintSpec" -- the one specialization front
    door. Walks the template's trie level per definition-order
    parameter (type-id keys, literal-value keys for numeric
    parameters); a hit returns the existing instance, a miss
    materializes one via #~"mintMono" and links it into the
    trie with its ordered #~"genericArgs".]
  [#*: the instance walk -- a mono's method bodies are re-walked over
    the TEMPLATE's AST with the instance's arguments bound
    (#~"monoParamSubst"), stamping per-instance types into
    #~"monoNodeType"; nothing is cloned.]
  [#*: #~"validateGenericTypeArgs" -- enforces generic
    constraints on a candidate argument type.]

Node synthesis (the hoist temps, the generator lowering) is described
in #~"compiler.pdoc:Synthesis Toolkit".

#~"is native" methods (the compiler-provided collection methods
on #~"array", #~"List", #~"Map", #~"str", etc.) are #_*"not"
special-cased -- they are cloned alongside user methods. The
only requirement is that their declaration in
#~"lib/system/system.z" or #~"lib/system/collections.z" carries
the #~"is native" form. Resolution flows through the standard
child-lookup path.


#---: Type narrowing

Narrowing for #~"union" and #~"variant" subjects in match arms
is implemented via overlay entries. When a match arm narrows
subject #~"s" to subtype #~"ok", an overlay #~"ZEntry" with
#~"narrowedSubtype: \"ok\"" is pushed onto the arm's scope.
Inside the arm, name lookup for #~"s" finds this entry first and
returns the narrowed type. The arm scope is popped at end of
arm; narrowing resets automatically.

Post-match #_*"exclusion" is the dual: arms that diverge
(return, break, panic) contribute their handled subtypes to an
#~"excludedSubtypes" entry placed on the parent scope after the
match. Subsequent code that re-accesses #~"s" as an excluded
subtype is rejected.

Assignment-based narrowing (e.g. #~"x: Result.ok 42") places a
narrowing entry in the current scope at definition time;
reassignment resets it.

Narrowing and exclusion are carried as arm name ids on the overlay
row (#~"narrowedSubtype", #~"excludedSubtypes").


#---: Auto-call coercion

A no-user-arg method dotted path read in value position
(#~"v: s.stringview", #~"p1.distance" inside a string
interpolation) is auto-called to its return type. The coercion
lives entirely in #~"checkDotted" and is gated by a
coerce-to-return flag. #~"checkCall" clears the
flag when resolving its own callable, so explicit calls
(#~"obj.method named: arg") see the function type and dispatch
normally instead of constructing the return type. Path
resolution itself is context-free: it
always returns the FUNCTION type for a method path. Behaviour is
identical for #~"is native" and user-defined methods.


#---: Unified call dispatch

A type used in callable position is rewritten through the
unified-dispatch route. The bare-name construction call
#~"Point x: 1 y: 2" resolves the callee to the #~"Point"
#~"ZType", then looks up #~"children[create]" on that type to
get the actual constructor. The mechanics live in the return-call
dispatch; #~"meta.create" on the
parent #~"ZType" carries the compiler-generated raw allocator
when one is needed. #~"create: null" in the type's #~"as" block
disables bare-name construction (the case for #~"union" and
#~"variant" where subtype selection is required).

A bare type name in a runtime value position (no call at all) is the
zero-argument case of the same dispatch. #~"checkAtomid" (and the
unit-qualified leg of #~"checkDotted") stamps eligible atoms in the
#~"implicitCreate" side table (nodeid to constructed type id, beside
#~"callKind" / #~"atomUnitDefId"); the emitter keys the zero-value
emission off that stamp. Eligibility is validated up front --
#~"missingCtorFields" must come back empty -- and non-constructible
families get targeted E0300 diagnostics instead of a stamp. Two gates
keep the rule honest: the atom must #_*"name" the type (a value alias
like #~"true: bool.true" resolves to the bool type id but is a value
use), and #~"suppressBareCtor" spans dotted-path #_*"base" walks so
#~"Counter.create value: 99" does not construct its base atom.

The runtime-backed stdlib units (system/core/collections/io/os/cli)
implement their type methods in the C runtime: a type method with a
#~".z" body there is E0100, while bodied #_*"free" functions are legal --
they are resolved and body-walked like any other unit's, only when the
program's demand loop referenced them. See the compiler doc's
"Runtime-backed stdlib units".


#--: Error Reporting

Type errors are #~"zast.ErrorData" value objects, appended to
#~"ZTyping.errors" via the #~"addError" helper. Errors are never
raised as exceptions; the typechecker tries to continue
collecting errors past the first failure where the AST permits.
Error codes (the #~"err" variant in #~"lib/system/zast.z") are grouped:

#ul:
  [#*: E0001-E0099 -- parser / syntax (raised earlier in the
    pipeline)]
  [#*: E0100-E0199 -- type resolution]
  [#*: E0200-E0299 -- ownership]
  [#*: E0300-E0399 -- call / argument]
  [#*: E0400-E0499 -- generic / monomorphisation]

The diagnostic location is taken from the offending AST node's
#~"loc" / #~"Token". After #~"typecheck()" returns, the caller
inspects #~"ZTyping.errors" to decide whether to proceed to
emission.


#--: What Lives Elsewhere

Module boundaries the typechecker relies on:

#ul:
  [#*: #~"src/ztypes.z" -- #~"ZType", #~"ZVariable",
    #~"ZEntry", #~"ZTypeRegistry",
    plus the #~"ztypetype" / #~"zownership" /
    #~"zparamownership" / #~"zlockstate" / #~"zlockscope" /
    #~"zcontrolkind" / #~"zlockholderkind" / #~"zlockholder" enums,
    the numeric-literal parser and the shared mangler #~"mangleVarName"]
  [#*: #~"src/ztyping.z" -- the #~"ZTyping" container and the
    #~"Decl" tree]
  [#*: #~"src/zenv.z" -- #~"ZSymbolTable", the lock-conflict logic
    (#~"lockAcquireConflict", #~"pathsOverlap"), narrowing
    / exclusion installers]

Collection-type predicates (#~"typeIsReftype", #~"typeIsValtype",
...), node synthesis, and the monomorphisation dedup hashing have
no separate module -- they live within #~"src/ztypecheck.z" itself.


#--: Reading Order

When opening the typechecker for the first time, read in this
order:

#ol:
  [#*: #~"src/ztypes.z" -- the type definitions in this
    file are the vocabulary for everything else.]
  [#*: #~"src/ztyping.z" -- the #~"ZTyping" container makes the
    component-table architecture concrete.]
  [#*: #~"src/zenv.z" -- the scope stack and the lock-conflict
    rule. The semantics of "what makes two locks conflict" lives
    here, not in the typechecker.]
  [#*: #~"src/ztypecheck.z" -- enter via the Code Map above.
    A good first dive is #~"typecheck" → #~"resolveDef" →
    #~"resolveFunction" → #~"checkFunctionBody" →
    #~"checkStatement" → #~"checkExpression" →
    #~"checkCall". That path touches almost every cross-
    cutting concern in turn.]


